:r><a:rPr b="1" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>puromycin</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="400"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" cap="none" i="0" lang="en-US" strike="noStrike" sz="2000" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>made by the mold </a:t></a:r><a:r><a:rPr b="0" cap="none" i="1" lang="en-US" strike="noStrike" sz="2000" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Streptomyces alboniger</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="400"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" cap="none" i="0" lang="en-US" strike="noStrike" sz="2000" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="0000FF"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>similar structure to 3’-end of aminoacyl-tRNA</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="400"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" cap="none" i="0" lang="en-US" strike="noStrike" sz="2000" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>so it binds to the A site of ribosomes, forming bond with growing peptide</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-285750" lvl="1" marL="742950" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="400"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2000"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="–"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" cap="none" i="0" lang="en-US" strike="noStrike" sz="2000" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>but can’t participate in translocation and dissociation</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr algn="l" indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="480"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2400"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2400" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Thus, puromycin terminates protein synthesis.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:cxnSp><p:nvCxnSpPr><p:cNvPr id="581" name="Google Shape;581;p80"/><p:cNvCxnSpPr/><p:nvPr/></p:nvCxnSpPr><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="381000" y="838200"/><a:ext cx="8382000" cy="0"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="straightConnector1"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln cap="flat" cmpd="thinThick" w="57150"><a:solidFill><a:srgbClr val="2FB0DC"/></a:solidFill><a:prstDash val="solid"/><a:miter lim="800000"/><a:headEnd len="med" type="none" w="med"/><a:tailEnd len="med" type="none" w="med"/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:cxnSp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="Puromycin disruption of peptide bond formation.
This begins with the subunit structure previously described. This has one elongated intertwined green structure in the middle, with several blue circles extending up and left. In the A column of the large subunit is an elongated pinkish oval, labeled as puromycin. The green mRNA line goes through this like a bell curve. This originates with 5[prime] with a split in the line on the left. It ends with 3[prime]. Above this is a box as an inset, which is an enlargement of the blue circle and pinkish oval. In the bottom blue circle, it begins with C which triple bonds right to O and down to N. N bonds right to H, down to C which bonds right to R, left to H and down to C, which double bonds right to O and bonds left to O, which bonds down to the top left point of an inverted pentagon in a purple section of the box. The bond line between this C and O has a red arrow pointing from it to its O. This pentagon bonds up to HO at the top right point. The right point bonds down to a blue-filled pentagon bonded to a blue filled hexagon. The hexagon’s bottom point bonds down to NH2. The previous O also bonds down to O which bonds down to P in a whit circle. This structure indicates peptidyl-tRNA in P site.  Beginning back at the top and to the right is a pink oval labeled as puromycin in A site. This begins with OCH3, which bonds down to a benzene molecule, which bonds down to CH2, which bonds down to C. C bonds right to H and left to N, which bonds up to H and down to H. The N has two red dots and an arrow pointing to the bottom C in the bottom circle on the left. Back to the oval, C bonds down to C, which triple bonds right to O and bonds down to HN, which bonds down to the top left point of an inverted pentagon. The top right point bonds up to OH. The bottom left point bonds down, then left to O. The bottom point is O, and the right point bonds down to a blue-filled pentagon which is bonded to a hexagon on the left. The bottom point of the hexagon bonds down to N, which bonds down and left to H3C and bonds down and right to CH3. Below the subunit image is a down arrow, representing peptidyl transferase (blue), pointing down to a replica of the subunit image described above. From the blue circles is a line pointing to a larger version of them, and the bottom being the largest. It begins with C, which bonds left to H and right to R. C bonds up to N, which bonds right to H, and bonds up to AA in the circle just above. C also bonds down to C, which triple bonds left to O and right to the left-most N in the pinkish oval as described above. This oval is labeled as peptidyl puromycin. 
" id="582" name="Google Shape;582;p80"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="5181600" y="1066800"/><a:ext cx="2438400" cy="5632450"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>